      "title": "Cleaning up 'magic' quantum states cuts errors 40-fold",
      "summary": "Researchers ran a more efficient version of magic state distillation, a filtering step that removes flaws from special quantum resources, on real hardware for the first time and cut errors 40-fold. This matters because the traditional method needs huge numbers of qubits, and error-prone qubits are the main obstacle to useful quantum computers. It is still a first hardware test, and machines will need far lower overall error rates and many more qubits before this pays off at scale.",
